    "reproduces_published": true
  },
  "strengthens": [
    "The no-op arm reproduces the published qwen06b rows exactly -- identical count_a on 233 paired items and the same +92.1 point gap -- so the RAS arms differ from the paper's landed result in the sampling rule and in nothing else."
  ],
  "weakens": [
    "The printed repetition ratio sums from k = 0, which includes the token compared with itself; taken literally the rule fires when the candidate occurs at least once in the previous K, and that is what was run (48.8% of steps on repeated items). Under the other reading (sum from k = 1, so at least twice) it would have fired on 25.1% of steps. Only the literal reading drove generation.",
    "The fallback branch is the literal reading of 'random sampling': the untruncated, untempered model distribution. That is the flattest available reading and so the one most able to escape a loop; a tempered fallback would fire the same but escape less.",
    "VALL-E 2 sweeps top-p from 0.0 to 0.8; we ran two points, the checkpoint's shipped nucleus and v = 0.0. A minimum of the gap somewhere inside that interval is not excluded by these arms.",
    "One checkpoint. RAS was tested on Qwen3-TTS-0.6B only, for the reasons in src/common/ras.py; it is not a claim about RAS on VALL-E 2's own architecture, which we do not have.",
    "Ours is an implementation of a published description, not the authors' code, which was not released. Section 'WHAT WE IMPLEMENTED' in src/common/ras.py lists every decision the description leaves open."
  ]
